<Sheet Background="" DeveloperKey="178" HasParamBitmap="True" Name="Joins" ParamBitmap="" Showpageaxis="True" Title="&amp;Date&#x000d;&#x000a;&amp;Sheet (&amp;File)&#x000d;&#x000a;&apos;One to Many&apos; or &apos; One to One&apos; Joins, by Business Area&#x000d;&#x000a;&#x000d;&#x000a; The Master/Detail relationship is exposed in the Worksheet below. An &apos;Outer Join&apos; is displayed as &apos;(+)&apos; symbol, next to the folder name on either the Master or Detail side, as appropriate. To see the underlying database column &apos;Drill&apos; on either the &apos;Folder Name&apos; or &apos;Item Name&apos;.&#x000d;&#x000a; &#x000d;&#x000a;Note: Joins effect the use of Summaries in a query. If a foreign key join is NOT mandatory (i.e.&apos;Foreign Key Mandatory = NO&apos;. -  It can have null values) and is then used in the creation of a Summary, it will effect Summary Redirection. &#x000d;&#x000a; &#x000d;&#x000a;E.g. If the Administrator generates a Summary, selecting  items from two separate Folders, using the Outer Join (&apos;Join can have null values&apos;). A Summary will be generated as normal.  Then if a user, when running a query, selects Items from both Folders that are in the Summary, Summary redirection will occur as expected. However, if a user, when running a query select items, from only one of the Folders, then Summary Redirection will NOT occur and the Summary will not be used. This is because,  the Join in the users query is not being used. The results held in the Summary used the Join. The Summary did not summarize the rows where the foreign key values were null. Hence the Summary cannot be used as it would give false results. This type of join is sometimes referred to as a &apos;Losses Join&apos; (Please refer to the documentation for further detailed explanation)." TitleAlign="Tiled" TitleBackColor="32896" TitleBitmapReference="" TitleDeveloperKey="245" TitleHtml="&lt;table width=&quot;100%&quot; border=0 cellpadding=&quot;2&quot; cellspacing=&quot;0&quot;&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td bgcolor=#808000&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;p align=right&gt;&lt;font size=2 face=&quot;Arial&quot; color=#feffff&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;Date&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td bgcolor=#808000&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;p align=center&gt;&lt;font size=2 face=&quot;Arial&quot; color=#feffff&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;i&gt;&lt;u&gt;&amp;Sheet (&amp;File)&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td bgcolor=#808000&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;p align=left&gt;&lt;font size=2 face=&quot;Arial&quot; color=#feffff&gt;&lt;b&gt;&apos;One to Many&apos; or &apos; One to One&apos; Joins, by Business Area&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td bgcolor=#808000&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;p align=left&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td bgcolor=#808000&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;p align=left&gt;&lt;font size=2 face=&quot;Arial&quot; color=#feffff&gt; The Master/Detail relationship is exposed in the Worksheet below. An &apos;Outer Join&apos; is displayed as &apos;(+)&apos; symbol, next to the folder name on either the Master or Detail side, as appropriate. To see the underlying database column &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td bgcolor=#808000&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;p align=left&gt;&lt;font size=2 face=&quot;Arial&quot; color=#feffff&gt;&apos;Drill&apos; on either the &apos;Folder Name&apos; or &apos;Item Name&apos;.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td bgcolor=#808000&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;p align=left&gt;&lt;font size=2 face=&quot;Arial&quot; color=#feffff&gt; &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td bgcolor=#808000&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;p align=left&gt;&lt;font size=2 face=&quot;Arial&quot; color=#feffff&gt;Note: Joins effect the use of Summaries in a query. If a foreign key join is NOT mandatory (i.e.&apos;Foreign Key Mandatory = NO&apos;. -  It can have null values) and is then used in the creation of a Summary, it will effect Summary &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td bgcolor=#808000&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;p align=left&gt;&lt;font size=2 face=&quot;Arial&quot; color=#feffff&gt;Redirection. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td bgcolor=#808000&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;p align=left&gt;&lt;font size=2 face=&quot;Arial&quot; color=#feffff&gt; &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td bgcolor=#808000&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;p align=left&gt;&lt;font size=2 face=&quot;Arial&quot; color=#feffff&gt;E.g. If the Administrator generates a Summary, selecting  items from two separate Folders, using the Outer Join (&apos;Join can have null values&apos;). A Summary will be generated as normal.  Then if a user, when running a query, selects &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td bgcolor=#808000&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;p align=left&gt;&lt;font size=2 face=&quot;Arial&quot; color=#feffff&gt;Items from both Folders that are in the Summary, Summary redirection will occur as expected. However, if a user, when running a query select items, from &lt;i&gt;only&lt;/i&gt; one of the Folders, then Summary Redirection will &lt;i&gt;NOT&lt;/i&gt; occur and the &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td bgcolor=#808000&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;p align=left&gt;&lt;font size=2 face=&quot;Arial&quot; color=#feffff&gt;Summary will not be used. This is because,  the Join in the users query is not being used. The results held in the Summary used the Join. The Summary did &lt;i&gt;not &lt;/i&gt;summarize the rows where the foreign key values were null. Hence &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td bgcolor=#808000&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;p align=left&gt;&lt;font size=2 face=&quot;Arial&quot; color=#feffff&gt;the Summary cannot be used as it would give false results. This type of join is sometimes referred to as a &apos;Losses Join&apos; &lt;i&gt;(Please refer to the documentation for further detailed explanation).&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;&lt;/table&gt;&#x000d;&#x000a;" TitleRtf="{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang2057{\fonttbl{\f0\fswiss Arial;}{\f1\fswiss\fcharset0 Arial;}{\f2\fswiss\fprq2 System;}}&#x000d;&#x000a;{\colortbl ;\red254\green255\blue255;}&#x000d;&#x000a;\viewkind4\uc1\pard\qr\cf1\b\f0\fs18 &amp;Date&#x000d;&#x000a;\par \pard\qc\ul\i &amp;Sheet (&amp;File)&#x000d;&#x000a;\par \pard\ulnone\i0 &apos;One to Many&apos; or &apos; One to One&apos; Joins, by Business Area&#x000d;&#x000a;\par \b0 &#x000d;&#x000a;\par  The \f1 M\f0 aster/\f1 D\f0 etail relationship is exposed in the \f1 W\f0 orksheet below. An &apos;Outer Join&apos; is displayed as &apos;(+)&apos; symbol, next to the folder name on either the Master or Detail side, as appropriate. To see the underlying database column &apos;Drill&apos; on either the &apos;Folder Name&apos; or &apos;Item Name&apos;.&#x000d;&#x000a;\par  &#x000d;&#x000a;\par Note: Joins effect the use of \f1 S\f0 ummaries in a query. If a foreign key join is NOT mandatory (i.e.&apos;Foreign Key Mandatory = NO&apos;. -  It can have null values) and is then used in the creation of a \f1 S\f0 ummary, it will effect \f1 S\f0 ummary \f1 R\f0 edirection. &#x000d;&#x000a;\par  &#x000d;&#x000a;\par E.g. If the \f1 A\f0 dministrator generates a \f1 S\f0 ummary, selecting  items from two separate \f1 F\f0 olders, using the \f1 O\f0 uter \f1 J\f0 oin (&apos;Join can have null values&apos;). A \f1 S\f0 ummary will be generated as normal.  Then if a user, when running a query, selects \f1 I\f0 tems from both \f1 F\f0 olders that are in the \f1 S\f0 ummary, \f1 S\f0 ummary redirection will occur as expected. However, if a user, when running a query select items, from \i only\i0  one of the \f1 F\f0 olders, then \f1 S\f0 ummary \f1 R\f0 edirection will \i NOT\i0  occur and the \f1 S\f0 ummary will not be used. This is because,  the \f1 J\f0 oin in the users query is not being used. The results held in the \f1 S\f0 ummary used the \f1 J\f0 oin. The \f1 S\f0 ummary did \i not \i0 summari\f1 z\f0 e the rows where the foreign key values were null. Hence the \f1 S\f0 ummary cannot be used as it would give false results. This type of join is sometimes referred to as a &apos;Losses Join&apos; \i (Please refer to the documentation for further detailed explanation).\f2\fs20 &#x000d;&#x000a;\par }&#x000d;&#x000a;" UniqueName="{5A751726-D200-11D5-B05B-00C04FEDB835}">
